EXPERIMENT (not a proposed fix): skip bRequest 0x03 in verify cleanup

This patch exists to answer one question and should not be shipped without
the answer. Apply it on top of 0002.

After a capture, the a900 stops answering bRequest 0x03, the host cancels the
control transfer after 1 s, and ~80 ms later the sensor DISCONNECTS from the
USB bus and re-enumerates (FINDINGS.md 13.9). Three hypotheses for the
trigger, which the existing traces cannot separate because every run cancels
at exactly the same offset:

  H1  the device resets itself on its own timer after a capture
  H2  the host's cancellation of the in-flight EP0 transfer triggers it
  H3  the capture's bulk traffic does, via the device's non-compliant
      high-speed endpoint descriptor (wMaxPacketSize 64, must be 512)

Removing the transfer entirely resolves all three at once, because then no
command is sent and nothing is cancelled:

  - no disconnect            -> 0x03-post-capture is the trigger (H1 via 0x03,
                                or H2); enrollment may now proceed
  - disconnect still at
    ~+1080 ms after the last
    image record              -> the capture itself resets the device (H3, or
                                H1 on its own timer). A complete negative
                                result.

This is safe: it sends strictly FEWER commands to the device than the current
code, and no new or speculative ones.

Note this removes 0x03 only from the verify/identify cleanup state. Init's
0x03 call site is untouched and still runs -- it is acknowledged in ~2 ms
there and is presumably required.

The shape is not invented: FP_ENROLL_DISCARD, which is the enroll SSM's
cleanup state, already does exactly this and nothing else.
---
--- a/libfprint/drivers/fpcmoh/fpc.c
+++ b/libfprint/drivers/fpcmoh/fpc.c
@@ -987,9 +987,10 @@
 
     case FP_VERIFY_CANCEL:
       {
-        /* Cleanup state -- see fpc_verify_cleanup_cb. A device that declines
-         * to answer this must not sink an operation that already succeeded. */
-        fpc_write_ctrl (ssm, device, 0x03, 0x01, NULL, 0, fpc_verify_cleanup_cb, NULL);
+        /* EXPERIMENT (patches/0003): do not send 0x03 from cleanup at all.
+         * Mirrors FP_ENROLL_DISCARD, which is the enroll SSM's cleanup state
+         * and already does exactly this. See FINDINGS.md 13.10. */
+        fpi_ssm_next_state (ssm);
       }
       break;
     }
